    "disorder": {
      "word": "DISORDER",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Want of order or regular disposition; lack of arrangement; confusion; disarray; as, the troops were thrown into disorder; the papers are in disorder.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Neglect of order or system; irregularity. From vulgar bounds with brave disorder part, And snatch a grace beyond the reach of art. Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Breach of public order; disturbance of the peace of society; tumult. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Disturbance of the functions of the animal economy of the soul; sickness; derangement. \"Disorder in the body.\" Locke. disturbance; disease; illness; indisposition; sickness; ailment; malady; distemper. See Disease.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To disturb the order of; to derange or disarrange; to throw into confusion; to confuse. Disordering the whole frame or jurisprudence. Burke. The burden . . . disordered the aids and auxiliary rafters into a common ruin. Jer. Taylor.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To disturb or interrupt the regular and natural functions of (either body or mind); to produce sickness or indisposition in; to discompose; to derange; as, to disorder the head or stomach. A man whose judgment was so much disordered by party spirit. Macaulay.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To depose from holy orders. [Obs.] Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
